<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bauhaus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bauhaus was a German school of art, design and architecture founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-gropius">Walter Gropius</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> in 1919. It sought to unite crafts and fine arts and became a leading centre of modernist design, with teachers such as Paul Klee, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassily-kandinsky">Wassily Kandinsky</a> and László Moholy-Nagy. It moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> in 1925, into a building designed by Gropius, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1932, before closing under Nazi pressure in 1933.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bauhaus.jpg" alt="Bauhaus" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Spyrosdrakopoulos · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:6265_Dessau.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
